      "id": 1,
      "text": "Beckley, founded April 4 1838, grew around coal mining and became a regional hub for the industry.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beckley%2C_West_Virginia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The city was named for John James Beckley, the first Clerk of the House and first Librarian of Congress.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beckley%2C_West_Virginia",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1850, the Virginia legislature made Beckley the county seat of the newly formed Raleigh County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beckley%2C_West_Virginia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Alfred Beckley settled nearby in 1838, turning the town into a historic lodging and overland transportation hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://wvexplorer.com/communities/cities-towns/beckley-west-virginia/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Beckley Exhibition Coal Mine, a former mine turned tourist site, is listed on the National Register of Historic Places.",
